
  [1mC. Ephemeral, watermark only[0m
  Timers fire events into the bus; notifications live in memory only; [38;5;153mlastSeenAt[39m is the sole persisted state. Cheapest.
  [3mCosts:[0m dismissals don't survive a reload, so a notification you dismissed comes back on refresh. That's the kind of
  small betrayal that makes a feature feel broken.

  [1mRecommendation: A.[0m The self-invalidating ID is the whole argument — it removes the single largest bug class in
  notification systems (stale notifications about state that changed) by construction rather than by reconciliation
  logic. It's also the most testable: the core is a pure function of [38;5;153m(tasks, now, lastSeenAt)[39m, so every scenario —
  overdue while away, rescheduled past its notification, dismissed then reloaded — is a table test with no clock mocking
  beyond passing a different [38;5;153mnow[39m.

  B becomes the right answer the day a server generates events the client can't recompute. Migrating A→B then is
  additive, because the bus already separates production from consumption.

